# Worktree procedure

Read this before work that runs in a task worktree: every `spec` and `proposal` activity in `direct` mode under `[GIT_POLICY: worktree=required ...]`, and any task the `GIT_POLICY` levels in [git policy](git-policy.md) put in a worktree under `auto`. A request that names the checkout ("on main", "in this worktree") overrides the policy for that task only; record the deviation as a user request in the final change report's assumptions.

- `<base>` is the checkout the request arrived in; `<branch>` is `plan-<slug>` or `dev-<slug>` ([branches](branches.md)).
- Policy is read from the checkout a command runs in, so a running branch sees a base policy change only after merging the base.

## 1. Start (in `<base>`, before any bundle or edit)

- `proposal` Planning: `devkit worktree start plan-<slug>` and the printed `cd`; the proposal bundle is the unit.
- `proposal` Development: present the accepted proposal, report and worktree/setup effects, obtain conversational approval under `ask`, then run `devkit spec proposal handoff <slug> --report-uri <uri> --effort low|medium|high` from the retained base. Under `auto`, the command reads `proposal-handoff` from the base and uses the declared `dev_effort`.
  - Continue in the returned worktree path with the selected effort; run `devkit workflow next --unit <dir>` and the post-handoff link check.
  - On a partial result, inspect `devkit worktree status` and preserve the created worktree for recovery.
- `spec` depth: `devkit worktree start dev-<slug>`, the printed `cd`, then `devkit spec new <slug>` (`--workflow sdd-spec-bugfix` for a defect); record `plan-intent` and commit.
- `session` depth bound for a PR: `dev-<slug>`, in a worktree only when the `GIT_POLICY` levels call for one, else `--no-worktree`; no bundle.
- On `WORKTREE_BRANCH_EXISTS`, continue the branch ([branches](branches.md) §3) or choose another slug.

## 2. Work (in the worktree)

Run the workflow's stages, checking each boundary with `devkit workflow next --unit <dir>`, and commit on `<branch>` at every stage boundary. Push nothing and merge nothing into the base before `dev-integration`.

## 3. Integrate and finish (in `<base>`)

Integrate with `spec-integrate`; it owns the merge routes and their recovery. Finishing the worktree adds:

- Through a PR: after the merge, run `devkit worktree finish <branch>` (no `--merge`). A PR merged by squash or rebase stays `WORKTREE_UNMERGED`; remove that worktree and branch only with the user's authorization.
- A conflict in `.devkit/installation.json` when integrating the base: keep either side, run `devkit sync` and stage the receipt it writes.
- On `POST_FINISH_EXTENSION_FAILED`, resolve the cause, then run `devkit spec scope sweep --apply` and `devkit spec proposal prune <slug>`. After local integration has already landed, `devkit worktree finish <branch> --local-only` validates and performs only remaining guarded cleanup.
- A refused finish runs no cleanup and leaves the task registered; `devkit spec scope release -w <worktree path>` withdraws an abandoned task.
- `WORKTREE_DIRTY`: commit or discard in the worktree, never remove it by force. `WORKTREE_LOCKED`, and `WORKTREE_NOT_FOUND` for a merged branch devkit did not create: act only once the user agrees.

Leftover and stale worktrees and branches: [branches](branches.md) §4.
